Riigikogu: E.Essia wants to make climate targets for 2050 even more ambitious
Anneli Ott, the chairman of the commission, noted that to date there have been three hearings in the commission.
Experts, Entrepreneurs and representatives of the Ministry of the Environment presented their vision for achieving carbon neutrality in Estonia.
“Received review will allow us to argue that, in conjunction with other EU member states, Estonia is actively looking for opportunities to make climate goals for 2050 even more ambitious and to agree on ways to achieve them,” Ott said.
The Chairman added that the government has also been planning to discuss an analysis of Estonia's increase of climate ambitions on October 3.
Judgment, made on its results, will be further negotiated in the Riigikogu commissions.
Vice-President of the Commission Riyna Sikkut said that Estonia needs a clear plan to achieve climate goals, negotiated with all interested parties.
An international agreement and a high cost of carbon dioxide emissions quotas will in any case.
“Instead of energy, as part of intra-state discussions of the Riigikogu could focus on measures in agriculture, transport, timber and housing.
It is necessary to take into account the socio-economic impact of all the changes,” Sikkut said.
This autumn, the Commission of the Riigikogu on European Union Affairs launched a series of parliamentary hearings to prepare a decision with which Estonia will take part in the negotiations of the EU on climate targets for 2050.
